1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,603
After the rain, a pond at night.

2
00:00:02,603 --> 00:00:04,419
Loud croaking from the water.

3
00:00:04,419 --> 00:00:06,018
A green frog on a leaf.

4
00:00:06,418 --> 00:00:09,966
The croaking comes from male
frogs, puffing up pouches of skin.

5
00:00:10,416 --> 00:00:12,154
Diya picked one up gently.

6
00:00:12,154 --> 00:00:14,183
Its skin was cool and slippery.

7
00:00:14,183 --> 00:00:18,400
She wants to be a doctor, so she
asked: does a frog drink water?

8
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:21,800
And what inside it lets it
live in water and on land?

9
00:00:22,150 --> 00:00:24,432
Two parts of the chapter answer that.

10
00:00:24,432 --> 00:00:26,188
Morphology, its outer form.

11
00:00:26,188 --> 00:00:28,134
And anatomy, its inner organs.

12
00:00:28,634 --> 00:00:32,547
By the end, you will know the frog's
skin, limbs and sense organs, and

13
00:00:32,547 --> 00:00:36,324
its digestion, breathing, heart,
excretion, brain and reproduction.

14
00:00:36,974 --> 00:00:38,369
First, meet the frog.

15
00:00:38,369 --> 00:00:40,598
The common Indian frog is Rana tigrina.

16
00:00:40,948 --> 00:00:42,997
Frogs are cold-blooded, or poikilotherms.

17
00:00:42,997 --> 00:00:46,082
Their body temperature changes
with the temperature around them.

18
00:00:46,532 --> 00:00:48,894
They can change colour
to hide from enemies.

19
00:00:48,894 --> 00:00:50,007
This is camouflage.

20
00:00:50,007 --> 00:00:53,200
And they hide in deep burrows
through peak summer and winter.

21
00:00:53,200 --> 00:00:54,724
Summer sleep is aestivation.

22
00:00:54,724 --> 00:00:56,247
Winter sleep is hibernation.

23
00:00:56,897 --> 00:00:59,918
Let's zoom into the
frog, from the outside.

24
00:01:00,268 --> 00:01:02,431
Its body has a head and a trunk.

25
00:01:02,431 --> 00:01:04,398
There is no neck and no tail.

26
00:01:04,898 --> 00:01:06,192
Zoom to the head.

27
00:01:06,192 --> 00:01:08,675
Above the mouth, a pair of nostrils.

28
00:01:08,675 --> 00:01:13,147
The eyes bulge out, and a nictitating
membrane covers them to protect them

29
00:01:13,147 --> 00:01:14,047
in water.

30
00:01:14,439 --> 00:01:18,881
On either side of the eyes is the
tympanum, a round ear that receives sound.

31
00:01:19,381 --> 00:01:20,339
Now the skin.

32
00:01:20,339 --> 00:01:23,623
It is smooth and slippery
with mucus, and always moist.

33
00:01:23,623 --> 00:01:25,353
The frog never drinks water.

34
00:01:25,353 --> 00:01:27,403
It absorbs water through its skin.

35
00:01:28,053 --> 00:01:31,432
Now its limbs and the
two sexes, step by step.

36
00:01:31,782 --> 00:01:32,682
Step one.

37
00:01:32,475 --> 00:01:36,295
Forelimbs and hind limbs help the
frog swim, walk, leap and burrow.

38
00:01:37,045 --> 00:01:37,945
Step two.

39
00:01:37,753 --> 00:01:39,707
The forelimbs end in four digits.

40
00:01:39,707 --> 00:01:43,623
The hind limbs are larger and more
muscular, and end in five digits.

41
00:01:44,373 --> 00:01:45,273
Step three.

42
00:01:45,200 --> 00:01:49,356
The hind feet are webbed, with skin
between the digits, for swimming.

43
00:01:50,106 --> 00:01:51,006
Step four.

44
00:01:50,852 --> 00:01:52,515
Frogs show sexual dimorphism.

45
00:01:52,515 --> 00:01:54,401
Males and females look different.

46
00:01:55,151 --> 00:01:56,051
Step five.

47
00:01:55,995 --> 00:02:00,507
A male has vocal sacs that make sound,
and a copulatory pad on the first

48
00:02:00,507 --> 00:02:02,098
digit of each forelimb.

49
00:02:02,098 --> 00:02:03,592
A female has neither.

50
00:02:04,342 --> 00:02:05,242
Remember this.

51
00:02:05,225 --> 00:02:06,876
Forelimbs end in four digits.

52
00:02:06,876 --> 00:02:08,280
Hind limbs end in five.

53
00:02:08,880 --> 00:02:09,780
Now inside.

54
00:02:09,679 --> 00:02:11,557
Follow a meal through the frog.

55
00:02:11,907 --> 00:02:14,877
The tongue is bilobed,
split in two at the tip.

56
00:02:14,877 --> 00:02:16,264
It catches an insect.

57
00:02:16,264 --> 00:02:20,387
Food goes from the mouth into the
buccal cavity, then through the pharynx

58
00:02:20,387 --> 00:02:22,323
and oesophagus into the stomach.

59
00:02:22,773 --> 00:02:27,276
The stomach and intestine digest it,
helped by bile from the liver, stored

60
00:02:27,276 --> 00:02:30,456
in the gall bladder, and
juice from the pancreas.

61
00:02:30,906 --> 00:02:33,322
The gut is short, because
frogs are carnivores.

62
00:02:33,322 --> 00:02:35,419
Undigested waste leaves
through the cloaca.

63
00:02:36,069 --> 00:02:36,999
Now breathing.

64
00:02:36,999 --> 00:02:39,233
A frog breathes in two different ways.

65
00:02:39,583 --> 00:02:42,526
In water, the skin is the breathing organ.

66
00:02:42,526 --> 00:02:44,896
Oxygen passes through the wet skin.

67
00:02:44,896 --> 00:02:46,895
This is cutaneous respiration.

68
00:02:47,345 --> 00:02:51,587
On land, it breathes through the
buccal cavity, the skin and a pair of

69
00:02:51,587 --> 00:02:54,481
lungs, pink sac-like
organs in the upper trunk.

70
00:02:54,931 --> 00:02:58,172
And during aestivation and hibernation,
gases are exchanged through the

71
00:02:58,172 --> 00:02:59,072
skin.

72
00:02:59,244 --> 00:03:03,469
The frog's heart has three chambers:
two atria above, and one ventricle

73
00:03:03,469 --> 00:03:04,369
below.

74
00:03:04,046 --> 00:03:06,654
A membrane, the pericardium, covers it.

75
00:03:07,004 --> 00:03:10,684
Blood flows in closed vessels,
so circulation is closed.

76
00:03:10,684 --> 00:03:13,616
And a frog's red blood
cells have a nucleus.

77
00:03:14,066 --> 00:03:16,997
To remove waste, a pair of
kidneys filter the blood.

78
00:03:16,997 --> 00:03:19,334
The frog makes urea, so it is ureotelic.

79
00:03:19,334 --> 00:03:21,456
Ureters carry the urine to the cloaca.

80
00:03:22,056 --> 00:03:22,956
Your turn.

81
00:03:22,658 --> 00:03:24,684
How many ventricles does
a frog's heart have?

82
00:03:24,684 --> 00:03:25,584
One, or two?

83
00:03:25,466 --> 00:03:26,366
Pause and decide.

84
00:03:29,551 --> 00:03:30,451
One!

85
00:03:30,039 --> 00:03:33,331
Two atria and a single
ventricle make three chambers.

86
00:03:33,981 --> 00:03:35,476
Now, a worked example.

87
00:03:35,476 --> 00:03:37,432
The frog's brain has three parts.

88
00:03:37,432 --> 00:03:39,122
Let's name what is in each.

89
00:03:39,472 --> 00:03:43,446
The forebrain has the olfactory lobes
for smell, the cerebral hemispheres

90
00:03:43,446 --> 00:03:44,765
and the diencephalon.

91
00:03:45,215 --> 00:03:48,650
The midbrain has a pair
of optic lobes, for sight.

92
00:03:49,100 --> 00:03:53,162
The hindbrain has the cerebellum and
the medulla oblongata, which continues

93
00:03:53,162 --> 00:03:54,534
into the spinal cord.

94
00:03:54,984 --> 00:03:58,434
The brain sits safe inside
a bony box, the cranium.

95
00:03:58,434 --> 00:04:01,224
Ten pairs of cranial nerves arise from it.

96
00:04:01,874 --> 00:04:03,336
Now, the exam trap.

97
00:04:03,686 --> 00:04:06,650
Many students think a frog's eggs
are fertilised inside the body.

98
00:04:07,050 --> 00:04:09,941
But fertilisation in frogs
is external, in water.

99
00:04:09,941 --> 00:04:13,827
The young hatch as tadpoles, and
change into frogs by metamorphosis.

100
00:04:14,477 --> 00:04:15,839
Where is this asked?

101
00:04:15,839 --> 00:04:19,861
As statement questions on the frog's
heart, excretion, fertilisation and

102
00:04:19,861 --> 00:04:21,022
sexual dimorphism.

103
00:04:21,622 --> 00:04:22,783
Exam level now.

104
00:04:22,783 --> 00:04:24,495
Read the four statements.

105
00:04:24,495 --> 00:04:26,108
Which ones are correct?

106
00:04:26,108 --> 00:04:27,319
Pause and solve.

107
00:04:32,719 --> 00:04:34,021
The answer is D.

108
00:04:34,021 --> 00:04:35,778
Statements one and three.

109
00:04:36,278 --> 00:04:37,313
Two is wrong.

110
00:04:37,313 --> 00:04:40,062
Frogs excrete urea, so they are ureotelic.

111
00:04:40,062 --> 00:04:41,145
Four is wrong.

112
00:04:41,145 --> 00:04:43,492
Fertilisation is external, in water.

113
00:04:43,992 --> 00:04:45,833
One and three are right.

114
00:04:45,833 --> 00:04:50,271
Two atria and one ventricle, and
the male has a copulatory pad.

115
00:04:50,871 --> 00:04:52,413
Here is your recap.

116
00:04:52,413 --> 00:04:53,997
Screenshot this card.

117
00:04:57,197 --> 00:04:59,383
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

118
00:04:59,783 --> 00:05:00,683
One.

119
00:05:00,201 --> 00:05:02,104
How does a frog breathe in water?

120
00:05:03,004 --> 00:05:03,904
Two.

121
00:05:03,367 --> 00:05:05,376
How many chambers does
a frog's heart have?

122
00:05:06,276 --> 00:05:07,176
Three.

123
00:05:06,780 --> 00:05:09,515
Name two ways to tell a
male frog from a female.

124
00:05:10,615 --> 00:05:14,010
Next time: a cockroach
runs before you even move.

125
00:05:14,010 --> 00:05:15,686
How does it sense you?

126
00:05:15,686 --> 00:05:19,081
We find out in cockroach,
body and organ systems.
